 "differences": [
  "The paper says 360 trials were recorded for each participant except participant 1 (192). Counting an attempt as completed when it reached the pursuit-tracking phase (marker 1004), 4 of the 14 participants have exactly 360 completed trials (others: 362, 361, 361, 363, 364, 361, 361, 364, 363, 362). ",
  "Observation-condition trials: 4 of 14 participants have exactly 180 (others have 182, 181, 181, 183, 184, 181, 181, 184, 183, 182).",
  "The authors' per-participant preprocessed data (outlier trials, EEG) are not part of the OSF deposit, so no trial is marked as an outlier; attempts that never reached the pursuit-tracking phase (marker 1004; aborted trials, which the authors re-queued) are marked excluded_by_authors = True; this is inferred from the event markers, not an exclusion list of the authors.",
  "The paper reports a grand average cursor movement onset of 3.23 s (SD 0.18) from trial start and a duration of 0.49 s (SD 0.13). Applying the paper's described onset method to the released cursor signal gives a mean of 3.26 s and 0.38 s here (mean of participant means, no outlier removal); these values are not released as columns and are not used as a check because the onset reference point is not fully specified.",
  "Cue codes 1 and 2 are not explained in the deposit; execution = 1 and observation = 2 was inferred from the palm excursion (large when 1, near zero when 2).",
  "Event codes other than the cue codes (1001-1004 and their negatives, 4xxx, 5xxx) are kept as the authors' numbers; the README names the pattern, not a verified meaning.",
  "The deposit has no eyeblock2.fdt for participant 9 (only its .set file), so that participant has one eye-block recording; every other participant has two."
 ],
